WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.240
Arthur Fleck's biggest joke just might be
the one he pulled on you.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.400
"Ah ha ha ha ha."

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.300
Joker director Todd Phillips recently made
a few very interesting comments to the Los

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.500
Angeles Times about the final scene of the
movie comments that imply a bizarre truth

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.180
about the story being presented.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.240
In case that's not warning enough, be advised
that major spoilers for Joker lie ahead.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.560
Joaquin Phoenix's Arthur is depicted early
and often as having a tenuous grip on reality.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.080
The film eventually lets on that it's not
always clear which events are fantasy and

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.220
which are reality not to the audience, and
presumably not to Arthur.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.260
At the end, Arthur is seen with his psychiatrist
in Arkham State Hospital after he has apparently

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.760
killed his mother, incited a riot, shot Murray
Franklin on live television, and escaped from

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.620
police custody with the help of his clown-masked
followers.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.580
As the shrink attempts to question him, Arthur
simply laughs, telling her that she wouldn't

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:58.360
get the joke.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.740
According to Phillips, it's this particular
laugh that might hold a clue about the true

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.940
nature of the story that just unfolded.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:05.780
He explained,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.960
"That laugh in that scene is really the only
time he laughs genuinely.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.780
There are different laughs in the movie.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.049
There is the laugh from Arthur's affliction
and then there is his fake laugh when he's

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:20.240
trying to be 'one of the people,' which is
my favorite laugh.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.740
But at the end, when he's in the room at Arkham
State Hospital, that's his only genuine laugh

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.460
in the movie."

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.740
Why is that, exactly?

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.560
Well, perhaps because in the end, the joke
is on us, the audience.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.900
Phillips has said that one of the main influences
on his film was The Killing Joke, the acclaimed

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.540
1988 graphic novel in which the Joker says
that if he must have a past, he, quote, "[prefers]

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.420
it to be multiple choice."

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.620
The director made a direct allusion to this
dialogue in suggesting that as many fans have

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.920
speculated Arthur Fleck might not necessarily
be who we think he is.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:55.940
Phillips said,

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.400
"There are lots of ways you could look at
this movie.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.900
You could look at it and go, 'This is just
one of his multiple-choice stories.

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:04.039
None of it happened.'

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.000
I don't want to say what it is.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.880
But a lot of people I've shown it to have
said, 'Oh, I get it he's just made up a story.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.640
The whole movie is the joke.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.940
It's this thing this guy in Arkham Asylum
concocted.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.920
He might not even be the Joker.'"

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.160
Phillips went on to state outright that the
man who will become Batman's nemesis may simply

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.920
be in the background somewhere, watching all
of these events play out.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.420
He said,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.240
"Maybe Joaquin's character inspired the Joker.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.420
You don't really know.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.080
His last line in the movie is, 'You wouldn't
get it.'

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.820
There's a lot going on in there that's interesting."

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.040
Phoenix himself also explained that Joker
can be seen not so much as a straightforward

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.480
narrative, but as a series of suggestions.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.200
During his own conversation with the Times,
he said,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.830
"This movie requires a certain amount of participation
from the audience.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.140
It's up to you how you want to interpret it
and experience it.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.160
It's less you being kind of presented with
the facts than you being presented with these

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.160
possibilities."

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.500
Phillips may have given the whole game away,
however, when he revealed that a face-off

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.440
with Batman would never be part of his potential
sequel to Joker, saying,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.920
"We would never do that.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:10.880
No, no.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.760
We'd just want to see where [Arthur] goes
from there."

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.620
"Do you see those two worlds merging together
anytime soon?

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.120
… No.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.660
Definitely not."

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.440
Taken together with Phillips' previous remarks,
we're left with the compelling possibility

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.840
that Arthur isn't really the Joker at least,
not the Joker who goes toe-to-toe with Batman.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.710
His entire rampage might have taken place
entirely in his head; perhaps he told the

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:38.190
story to a fellow Arkham inmate, who told
it to someone else, who passed it along until

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.260
it was eventually heard by the man who would
become the true Clown Prince of Crime.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.240
For now we can't say for sure, but don't worry
Phillips told Entertainment Tonight that eventually,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.520
he'll reveal the truth:

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:55.000
"One day I will say what we think and what
we meant when we were writing it."

